From 3d39ac2dd3e5664094fdd76e2296638653df35ae Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <noreply@invalid>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 12:20:11 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 42/56] nouveau/vk: nvkmd_horizon, say where a submit and a
 fence wait went

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 6 item 4 (Horizon WSI backend)
Why: "vkWaitForFences took N milliseconds" is one number, and at least
 four different things this backend does produce it. The syncpoint may
 genuinely have reached its threshold that late. There may have been no
 fence to wait on at all, so the wait sat in the D16 condition variable
 until a submit attached one - which says the caller waited on something
 nobody had submitted, not that anything was slow. A submit may have been
 blocked in nvkmd_horizon_ctx_wait doing on the CPU a wait belonging to
 another channel, with the queue held behind it. Or the fence may be far
 younger than the caller believes, so the wait is not the pipelined one
 it was written to be.
 .
 From outside the driver those are indistinguishable, and the ambiguity
 is blocking a diagnosis. An application presenting through
 VK_NN_vi_surface reports 157 ms per frame with 140.7 ms of it inside one
 vkWaitForFences on the fence of a submission it believes is three frames
 old, with an empty command buffer and no GPU work at all. This project's
 own hardware evidence disagrees by three orders of magnitude:
 t_vk_submits submits sixteen empty command buffers with fences and waits
 for them one at a time, and has measured 100, 115, 126 and 137 us per
 submit-to-signal round trip in four separate console runs. Nothing that
 can be measured from outside the application distinguishes the two, and
 this is the counter that can.
 .
 Per context, so the exec channel and the upload channel report
 separately rather than averaging into each other. A cross-channel wait
 therefore appears once as a CPU wait on the channel that performed it
 and once as a fence wait on the channel that owned the fence: two true
 statements about one event, not double counting inside either line.
 .
 Guarded, unlike the WSI acquire meter it is modelled on. That one is
 safe unlocked because vkAcquireNextImageKHR externally synchronises its
 swapchain; nothing externally synchronises this one - vkWaitForFences
 may run on any thread while a submit runs on another.
 .
 Only waits that blocked and then succeeded are counted. A wait that
 found the sync already signalled cost nothing and would dilute the mean;
 one that timed out measures the caller's patience rather than the fence.
 Same rule as the acquire meter, for the same reason.
 .
 Off by default: MESA_VK_NVKMD_HORIZON_SUBMIT_STATS, alongside the WSI
 backend's two. Left off it costs one branch per submit and per wait.
Evidence: cross build only. `scripts/build-mesa-nvk.sh` in the
 mesa-nvk-horizon/nx-dev-mesa image compiles nvkmd_horizon_ctx.c and
 nvkmd_horizon_sync.c and links libnvk.a clean against Mesa 26.1.6 with
 devkitA64, and the 33 test .nro relink; check-tls-relocs reports 0 TLS
 relocations in libnvk.a. NO CONSOLE HAS RUN THIS. The 157 ms and 140.7
 ms above are the reporter's application-side measurement, not this
 meter's output - the patch exists because that number cannot be
 attributed yet. The 100/115/126/137 us are this project's, from
 meter's output - the patch exists because that number cannot be
 -PASS.log.
Upstream: no - the meter is about this backend's channels, its D16
 condition variable and its CPU-side cross-channel wait, and the
 environment variable is named for it.
